LEFT JOIN works in the following way: it returns all rows from the left table (the first table in the query) plus all matching rows from the right table (the second table in the query).
RIGHT JOIN works in the following way: it returns all rows from the right table (the second table in the query) plus all matching rows from the left table (the first table in the query).
Another joining method is FULL JOIN. This type of JOIN returns all rows from both tables and combines the rows when there is a match. In other words, FULL JOIN is a union of LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN.
You can't, for example, put WHERE before FROM. Similarly, HAVING must always follow GROUP BY, not the other way around.
LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, and FULL JOIN are also shortcuts. They are all actually OUTER JOINS: LEFT OUTER JOIN, RIGHT OUTER JOIN, and FULL OUTER JOIN. You can add the keyword OUTER and the results of your queries will stay the same.

NATURAL JOIN and it's slightly different from the other methods because it doesn't require the ON clause with the joining condition - it always joins the two tables on the columns with the same name

